From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Sat, 20 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] build the Linux ANGLE libraries for riscv64

scripts/build_linux.py builds libEGL/libGLESv2 from the pinned ANGLE
revision for x86-64 and arm64 only, so there is no riscv64 wheel. Every
layer below it already knows the architecture:

  * depot_tools' detect_host_arch.py maps riscv* to riscv64, and CIPD
    publishes linux-riscv64 for the cipd client, cpython3, ninja and gn;
  * with is_clang=false Chromium's build selects
    //build/toolchain/linux:riscv64, a gcc_toolchain whose toolprefix is
    riscv64-linux-gnu, and config/compiler/BUILD.gn has riscv64 flags;
  * ANGLE's own gni/angle.gni lists riscv64 among the 64-bit CPUs, so it
    does not hit the "Unknown current CPU" assert.

So reuse the arm64 recipe rather than adding a new one. The arm64 path
already aliases the container's gcc/g++/ar/readelf/nm under the names
ANGLE's GCC toolchain expects and turns off Clang, the bundled libc++ and
warnings-as-errors; generalise that to a toolprefix table and add riscv64
to it. The build-time GN args are otherwise unchanged.

Two CIPD packages have no linux-riscv64 build and would abort
"gclient sync" before anything compiles: buildtools/reclient (which has no
linux-arm64 build either, hence the host_cpu carve-out already in DEPS) and
third_party/siso/cipd. Neither is used here - use_remoteexec is false, and
use_siso_default in //build/toolchain/siso.gni is false unless
build_with_chromium, which a standalone ANGLE checkout is not - so null
them out through the same custom_deps mechanism that already drops
SwiftShader, VK-GL-CTS and catapult.

gclient's helper scripts run under vpython3, which builds their venvs from
prebuilt wheels in CIPD, and infra/python/wheels publishes no linux-riscv64
builds - so "gclient sync" aborts with "no such package:
infra/python/wheels/crcmod/linux-riscv64_cp311_cp311" (and the same for
lxml) before any dependency is fetched. Set VPYTHON_BYPASS for that host so
those scripts run under the interpreter already on PATH, which is the
documented escape hatch for a manually managed Python.

The riscv64 libraries are compiled from source instead of being extracted
from an Electron release, so the wheel cannot carry Electron's
electron-LICENSE or the LICENSES.chromium.html that Electron's own build
generates. Stage the licences of the tree the libraries were actually built
from: ANGLE's LICENSE, plus an aggregate of the LICENSE/LICENCE/COPYING
files found under third_party/ in the checkout, which is what the
statically linked Vulkan loader, SPIRV-Tools, Abseil and friends require.
The aggregate is collected by directory rather than from the link line, so
it over-reports rather than risk omitting a component that is linked in.

manylinux_2_39 is the riscv64 baseline (the 2_28 images upstream uses for
x86-64 and arm64 are not published for riscv64), so that is the platform
tag the riscv64 wheel carries.

Both new tables are keyed by architecture, so x86-64 and arm64 render
byte-identical .gclient and GN args to before.

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not submitted: no cross-repo issue/PR contributions from this port]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
diff --git a/pyproject.toml b/pyproject.toml
--- a/pyproject.toml
+++ b/pyproject.toml
@@ -25,4 +25,10 @@
 include = ["comfy_angle*"]
 
 [tool.setuptools.package-data]
-comfy_angle = ["libs/*", "electron-LICENSE", "LICENSES.chromium.html"]
+comfy_angle = [
+    "libs/*",
+    "electron-LICENSE",
+    "LICENSES.chromium.html",
+    "LICENSE.angle",
+    "LICENSES.angle.txt",
+]
diff --git a/scripts/build.py b/scripts/build.py
--- a/scripts/build.py
+++ b/scripts/build.py
@@ -29,11 +29,17 @@
     "macosx_11_0_arm64",
     "manylinux_2_28_aarch64",
     "manylinux_2_28_x86_64",
+    "manylinux_2_39_riscv64",
     "win_amd64",
     "win_arm64",
 ]
 
-LICENSE_FILES = ["electron-LICENSE", "LICENSES.chromium.html"]
+LICENSE_FILES = [
+    "electron-LICENSE",
+    "LICENSES.chromium.html",
+    "LICENSE.angle",
+    "LICENSES.angle.txt",
+]
 FORBIDDEN_LINUX_LIBRARIES = {
     "libX11.so.6",
     "libXext.so.6",
diff --git a/scripts/build_linux.py b/scripts/build_linux.py
--- a/scripts/build_linux.py
+++ b/scripts/build_linux.py
@@ -19,7 +19,31 @@
 PLATFORM_TAGS = {
     "x86_64": ("x64", "manylinux_2_28_x86_64"),
     "aarch64": ("arm64", "manylinux_2_28_aarch64"),
-}
+    "riscv64": ("riscv64", "manylinux_2_39_riscv64"),
+}
+
+# Chromium publishes its prebuilt Clang only for x86-64 hosts, so the other
+# architectures build with the container's GCC, reached through ANGLE's
+# "<toolprefix>-<tool>" command names.
+NATIVE_GCC_TOOLPREFIX = {
+    "aarch64": "aarch64-linux-gnu",
+    "riscv64": "riscv64-linux-gnu",
+}
+
+# CIPD packages that do not exist for a host architecture. Both are unused for a
+# standalone ANGLE build (use_remoteexec and use_siso are false), so drop them
+# from the checkout rather than letting "gclient sync" fail on them.
+UNAVAILABLE_CIPD_DEPS = {
+    "riscv64": ("buildtools/reclient", "third_party/siso/cipd"),
+}
+
+# infra/python/wheels/* publishes no linux-riscv64 builds, so vpython3 cannot
+# assemble the venvs gclient's helper scripts ask for ("no such package:
+# infra/python/wheels/crcmod/linux-riscv64_cp311_cp311"). Run them under the
+# interpreter already on PATH instead, which is what VPYTHON_BYPASS is for.
+VPYTHON_BYPASS = {"riscv64"}
+
+LICENSE_GLOBS = ("LICENSE*", "LICENCE*", "COPYING*")
 
 FORBIDDEN_LIBRARIES = {
     "libX11.so.6",
@@ -66,24 +90,46 @@
         raise RuntimeError(f"{library.name} still depends on {names}")
 
 
-def configure_native_arm64_gcc(
-    work_dir: Path, env: Dict[str, str]
+def configure_native_gcc(
+    work_dir: Path, env: Dict[str, str], toolprefix: str
 ) -> Dict[str, str]:
-    """Expose the native compiler under ANGLE's ARM64 toolchain names."""
-    toolchain_dir = work_dir / "native-arm64-toolchain"
+    """Expose the native compiler under ANGLE's GCC toolchain names."""
+    toolchain_dir = work_dir / f"native-{toolprefix}-toolchain"
     toolchain_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
 
     for command in ("gcc", "g++", "ar", "readelf", "nm"):
         executable = shutil.which(command, path=env["PATH"])
         if executable is None:
             raise RuntimeError(f"Required build tool is unavailable: {command}")
-        alias = toolchain_dir / f"aarch64-linux-gnu-{command}"
+        alias = toolchain_dir / f"{toolprefix}-{command}"
         alias.unlink(missing_ok=True)
         alias.symlink_to(executable)
 
     configured = env.copy()
     configured["PATH"] = str(toolchain_dir) + os.pathsep + configured["PATH"]
     return configured
+
+
+def stage_licenses(angle: Path, destination: Path) -> None:
+    """Stage ANGLE's licence and those of the code linked into the libraries."""
+    shutil.copy2(angle / "LICENSE", destination / "LICENSE.angle")
+
+    sources = sorted(
+        {
+            path
+            for pattern in LICENSE_GLOBS
+            for path in angle.glob(f"third_party/**/{pattern}")
+            if path.is_file()
+        }
+    )
+    with (destination / "LICENSES.angle.txt").open("w") as aggregate:
+        aggregate.write(
+            "Licences of the third-party code in the ANGLE checkout that these\n"
+            "libraries are built from.\n"
+        )
+        for source in sources:
+            aggregate.write(f"\n{'=' * 78}\n{source.relative_to(angle)}\n{'=' * 78}\n")
+            aggregate.write(source.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"))
 
 
 def main() -> None:
@@ -111,6 +157,9 @@
     checkout(DEPOT_TOOLS_URL, depot_tools, depot_tools_revision)
     checkout(ANGLE_URL, angle, angle_revision)
 
+    unavailable_deps = "".join(
+        f"    '{dep}': None,\n" for dep in UNAVAILABLE_CIPD_DEPS.get(machine, ())
+    )
     (angle / ".gclient").write_text(
         "solutions = [{\n"
         "  'name': '.',\n"
@@ -121,6 +170,7 @@
         "    'third_party/SwiftShader': None,\n"
         "    'third_party/VK-GL-CTS/src': None,\n"
         "    'third_party/catapult': None,\n"
+        f"{unavailable_deps}"
         "  },\n"
         "  'custom_vars': {\n"
         "    'checkout_angle_cl_deps': False,\n"
@@ -134,11 +184,16 @@
     env = os.environ.copy()
     env["PATH"] = str(depot_tools) + os.pathsep + env["PATH"]
     env["DEPOT_TOOLS_UPDATE"] = "0"
+    if machine in VPYTHON_BYPASS:
+        env["VPYTHON_BYPASS"] = (
+            "manually managed python not supported by chrome operations"
+        )
     run(str(depot_tools / "ensure_bootstrap"), cwd=depot_tools, env=env)
     run("gclient", "sync", "--no-history", "--shallow", cwd=angle, env=env)
 
-    if machine == "aarch64":
-        env = configure_native_arm64_gcc(work_dir, env)
+    toolprefix = NATIVE_GCC_TOOLPREFIX.get(machine)
+    if toolprefix is not None:
+        env = configure_native_gcc(work_dir, env, toolprefix)
 
     out_dir = angle / "out" / "Release"
     gn_args_list = [
@@ -166,12 +221,12 @@
         "angle_enable_overlay=false",
         "angle_has_frame_capture=false",
     ]
-    if machine == "aarch64":
+    if toolprefix is not None:
         # Chromium only publishes its Linux Clang package for x86-64. ANGLE's
-        # GCC ARM64 toolchain works natively once the expected prefixed command
-        # names above resolve to the container's compiler. The bundled libc++
-        # requires GCC 15, while the manylinux image provides GCC 14, so use
-        # the image's libstdc++ and tolerate upstream's Clang-specific warnings.
+        # GCC toolchains work natively once the expected prefixed command names
+        # above resolve to the container's compiler. The bundled libc++ requires
+        # GCC 15, while the manylinux images provide GCC 14, so use the image's
+        # libstdc++ and tolerate upstream's Clang-specific warnings.
         gn_args_list.extend(
             [
                 "is_clang=false",
@@ -206,6 +261,8 @@
         verify_dependencies(target)
         libraries.append(target)
 
+    stage_licenses(angle, destination.parent)
+
     mode = getattr(ctypes, "RTLD_GLOBAL", 0)
     for library in libraries:
         ctypes.CDLL(str(library), mode=mode)
